# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: boethius-4.logic.r1 and boethius-4.logic.r1.2 have "license_removed → punishment_remitted"; boethius-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "that license which thou believest to be permitted to the wicked were taken away" is true, which the second allows
boethius-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps boethius-4.logic.r1 and boethius-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "that license which thou believest to be permitted to the wicked were taken away" is true and "the punishment of the wicked would be in great part remitted" is false. Is S2, "Yet if that license which thou believest to be permitted to them were taken away, the punishment of the wicked would be in great part remitted.", true then?
boethius-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: boethius-4.logic.r1 has "bad_more_unfortunate" and "bad_more_unfortunate → (license_removed → punishment_remitted)"; boethius-4.logic.r1.2 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3 have "bad_more_unfortunate"; they differ when only "the bad are more unfortunate when they have accomplished their desires than if they are unable to get them fulfilled" and "that license which thou believest to be permitted to the wicked were taken away" are true, which the second allows
boethius-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps boethius-4.logic.r1.2 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the bad are more unfortunate when they have accomplished their desires than if they are unable to get them fulfilled" and "that license which thou believest to be permitted to the wicked were taken away" are true and "the punishment of the wicked would be in great part remitted" is false. Is S3, "For verily, incredible as it may seem to some, it needs must be that the bad are more unfortunate when they have accomplished their desires than if they are unable to get them fulfilled.", true then?
boethius-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: boethius-4.logic.r1 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3 have "power_gives_effect" and "power_gives_effect → (will_evil_wretched → able_more_wretched)"; boethius-4.logic.r1.2 has "power_gives_effect"; they differ when only "it is wretched to will evil" and "without the power the wretched will would fail of effect" are true, which the second allows
boethius-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps boethius-4.logic.r1 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "it is wretched to will evil" and "without the power the wretched will would fail of effect" are true and "to have been able to accomplish evil is more wretched than only to will it" is false. Is S4, "If it is wretched to will evil, to have been able to accomplish evil is more wretched; for without the power the wretched will would fail of effect.", true then?
boethius-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 3 ways: boethius-4.logic.r1 has "each_state_wretched → threefold"; boethius-4.logic.r1.2 has "each_state_wretched"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; boethius-4.logic.r1.3 has "each_state_wretched" and "each_state_wretched → threefold"
boethius-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps boethius-4.logic.r1.2 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "each one of these states (willing, being able to accomplish, accomplishing crime) has its own measure of wretchedness" and "those who will, are able to accomplish, and accomplish crime are victims of a threefold wretchedness" are false. Is S5, "Accordingly, those whom thou seest to will, to be able to accomplish, and to accomplish crime, must needs be the victims of a threefold wretchedness, since each one of these states has its own measure of wretchedness.'", true then?
boethius-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: boethius-4.logic.r1 has "will_evil_wretched" and "will_evil_wretched ∧ able_more_wretched ∧ bad_more_unfortunate → each_state_wretched"; boethius-4.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the bad are more unfortunate when they have accomplished their desires than if they are unable to get them fulfilled", "it is wretched to will evil" and "to have been able to accomplish evil is more wretched than only to will it" are true, which the second allows; boethius-4.logic.r1.3 has "bad_more_unfortunate → (license_removed → punishment_remitted)"
boethius-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: boethius-4.logic.r1 takes for granted "it is wretched to will evil"; boethius-4.logic.r1.2 and boethius-4.logic.r1.3 do not
boethius-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-4.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "it is wretched to will evil"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
